# Pinned extra trust for the PDSP Ki download (tools/fetch/fetch_ki.py --download).
#
# What: the cross-certificate "emSign Root TLS CA - G1" (eMudhra) as ISSUED BY
# "emSign Root CA - G1", a root that IS in the Mozilla / system trust store.
#   subject : C=IN, O=eMudhra Technologies Limited, CN=emSign Root TLS CA - G1
#   issuer  : C=IN, OU=emSign PKI, O=eMudhra Technologies Limited, CN=emSign Root CA - G1
#   validity: 2024-07-11 .. 2039-07-09
#   SHA-256 : 9B:91:34:76:EA:B4:76:E2:1E:08:87:DB:CD:E4:43:45:D1:35:9D:28:11:6B:83:B7:CD:3E:87:BD:80:7E:BF:68
# Source: the CA Issuers (AIA) url of the InCommon intermediate,
#   http://repository.emsign.com/certs/EMIncommonOVG2C.p7c
# (transport does not matter: OpenSSL checks this cert's signature against the
# system-trusted emSign Root CA - G1 key, so a forged copy would simply not verify).
#
# Why: pdspdb.unc.edu serves leaf <- "InCommon Intermediate CA - OVG2C" <- "emSign Root
# TLS CA - G1", and that last root is not (yet) in the Mozilla store, so a stock client
# fails verification. With this cross-cert loaded as an extra CA, the chain completes up
# to the system root emSign Root CA - G1: full verification, hostname checking on, no
# partial-chain trust. If the server's chain rotates away from it, the download fails
# loudly pointing here: re-inspect the chain (openssl s_client -showcerts) before
# replacing this file, never disable verification.
